    resolution: "He takes the thread like it might bite, counts the knots twice, and pays in silver. \"The first when we clear the headland. The second if we must. The third—\" He shakes his head. \"Nobody looses the third.\" Then the practical half. Check the inshore waters forecast before you sail; the Isle of Man has its own area, and it comes out four times a day. Every man on deck wears his lifejacket: on a UK fishing boat it's been required since 2018, unless nothing on board could put a man over the side, and in the sea a man in a lifejacket is four times more likely to survive. Tell someone ashore where you're going and when you'll be back, so they can ring 999 and ask for the Coastguard if you're late. Carry a means of calling for help, and a personal locator beacon; in an emergency send a Mayday on Channel 16, or ring 999 and ask for the Coastguard. And if a man goes in, Float to Live: head back with the ears under, breathe, move the hands and feet, and let the cold shock pass.",
    lore: "Selling the wind was a real trade along the northern seaboard. In the fourteenth century Higden wrote of Man, in Trevisa's English: 'wommen there sellith schipmen wynde, as it were i-closed under thre knottes of threde; so that the more wynd he wol have, he wil unknette the mo knottes.' In Finland, as Frazer gives it from Olaus Magnus, the knots were graded: 'if they undid the first knot, a moderate wind sprang up; if the second, it blew half a gale; if the third, a hurricane.' He found the same 'in Lappland, in the island of Lewis, and in the Isle of Man', and 'Shetland seamen still buy winds from old women who claim to rule the storms' (1890). Shakespeare's witches trade them: 'Ile giue thee a Winde.' 'Th'art kinde' (Macbeth, 1623). Scott met one at Stromness in 1814, Bessy Millie, 'upwards of ninety', to whom each captain gave sixpence, 'and she boils her kettle to procure a favourable gale'; the next day he wrote, 'Bessy Millie's charm has failed us.'",
    tier: 'record',
    source: 'R. Higden, Polychronicon, trans. J. Trevisa, ed. C. Babington, vol. 2 (1869) · J. G. Frazer, The Golden Bough (1890), vol. 1, ch. I · Shakespeare, Macbeth, I.iii, First Folio (1623) · J. G. Lockhart, Memoirs of the Life of Sir Walter Scott, vol. III (1837) · HM Coastguard, "Onboard safety" · RNLI, "Angling" and "Float to Live" · MCA, MGN 588 (F) · Met Office, inshore waters forecast',
    reward: { gold: 70, xp: 40 },
