    resolution: "Before you go she cuts a lock of her hair and asks the aspen, through you, to shake in her stead. You bind the hair round the trunk, as they did in Lincolnshire, and bring her back a leaf, which trembles on the windowsill in no wind at all. The fits come further apart, and then they stop. Then the practical half. The ague was malaria, the fever that came back every other day; it has gone from England, and malaria isn't caught here now. But a high temperature, shivering and sweats in anyone who has been somewhere malaria is found, within the last 12 months, is an emergency: get an urgent GP appointment or ring 111 the same day, and say where you've been, because you could still have malaria a year after the trip. Before going to such a place, follow the ABCD: know the risk, avoid the bites with a 50% DEET repellent, a treated net, and arms and legs covered in the evening, take the antimalarial tablets, and get tested promptly if you fall ill. And a fever at home that isn't getting better, or is getting worse, means an urgent GP appointment or 111 too.",
    lore: "The aspen's leaves tremble in the faintest air, so it was asked to take a trembling illness. 'In Lincolnshire, a girl suffering from ague, cuts a lock of her hair, and binds it round an aspen-tree, praying it to shake in her stead' (Gordon Cumming, 1887), and Henderson (1879) indexes the aspen's 'charm for ague' beside the reason it trembles. That reason was told in many places: it 'was chosen by the enemies of Christ for the cross upon which to crucify the Saviour of mankind' (Carmina Gadelica, 1900), and 'The Aspen leaf is said to tremble because the cross was made of Aspenwood' (Brewer, 1894). The ague itself was malaria: 'The English word for malaria was ague, a term that remained in common usage until the 19th century' (Reiter, 2000). In the Fens it was endemic, 'a benign, tertian malaria, caused by Plasmodium vivax', its fever every other day (Nicholls, 2000), and it faded as the marshes were drained; the last case caught in England was in the 1950s.",
