    resolution: "The water shows the field by moonlight and something dancing a ring round the thorn. The pendulum swings hard. You tell Daly plainly: a lone thorn is the good people's, and the man who cuts one does not prosper. He grumbles, and ploughs around it, and at harvest sends you a sack of potatoes from the best crop that field has given in years. Then the practical half. A lone hawthorn in a field needs no felling licence, but the birds' law holds all year: it's an offence to wilfully take, destroy or damage the nest or eggs of a protected wild bird, or to disturb one on a nest with eggs or young in it, and a thorn in spring is full of nests. From 1 March to 31 August nothing growing in a hedge or a ditch, or on uncultivated land, may be cut, grubbed or burned, save for road safety and a few other exemptions. And if a tree must ever come down, the man with the saw should be trained for it, keep everyone else two tree-lengths away, and not work alone.",
    lore: "A lone hawthorn in a field was the good people's. 'A peasant would die sooner than cut down one of the ancient hawthorns sacred to the fairies', Lady Wilde wrote (1887). Allingham's fairies 'have planted thorn-trees / For pleasure here and there. / Is any man so daring / As dig them up in spite, / He shall find their sharpest thorns / In his bed at night' (in Yeats's anthology, 1888). A Kilmessan man in Meath told of a neighbour who boiled his potatoes over fairy-bushes he had cut; the wood 'would not burn', and 'In six months after cutting the fairy-bushes, he was dead' (Evans-Wentz, 1911). In the Isle of Man the thorn 'was regarded with veneration, there being a very strong prejudice against cutting it down' (Moore, 1891). Not everyone held to it: in Kerry in 1911 Edith Gordon had workmen fell one, and owned that any fear of what would follow 'was felt by me alone'. It lasts, too. In 1999 the storyteller Eddie Lenihan warned of misfortune if the Newmarket-on-Fergus bypass took the fairy bush at Latoon, and Clare County Council found 'it is possible to work around it'; its engineer wrote later that the road 'was not rerouted': the bush stood between the carriageway and a slip road, and was fenced in.",
    tier: 'record',
    source: 'Lady Wilde, Ancient Legends of Ireland (1887) · W. B. Yeats, ed., Fairy and Folk Tales of the Irish Peasantry (1888) · W. Y. Evans-Wentz, The Fairy-Faith in Celtic Countries (1911) · A. W. Moore, The Folk-Lore of the Isle of Man (1891), ch. VII · E. Gordon, Kerry Archaeological Magazine 6 (1911) · The Irish Times (29 May 1999; 19 March 2014) · Wildlife Act 1976, ss. 22, 40 · Forestry Act 2014, s. 19 · Health and Safety Authority, Basic Chainsaw Felling (2018)',
