    resolution: "You bind a sprig of white heather with white ribbon. In the cup you find a ring near the rim, and you are glad to be able to say so honestly. Kirsty cries a little. The bride carries the heather down the aisle and keeps it pressed in her Bible for forty years. Then the practical half. White heather is rare in the wild, and the foraging guidance says to pick only what is abundant and leave anything rare or unusual, so a nursery's white heather is the honest choice; if you do find it plentiful, take a sprig, never the plant: in Scotland it's an offence to uproot any wild plant without the landowner's permission, and nature reserves and SSSIs are to be left alone. And with Saturday so near, make sure the paperwork's in: in Scotland the marriage notices must reach the registrar no later than 29 days before the wedding, and they advise ten to twelve weeks.",
    lore: "White heather, a rare white-flowered form of the common purple, was lucky in Scotland. 'No Highlander would pass by it without picking it, for it is considered to bring good luck,' Queen Victoria wrote at Dunrobin (as Wallace quotes her, 1903). At Balmoral in September 1855 Prince Frederick William of Prussia, riding with the Princess Royal, 'picked a piece of white heather, (the emblem of \"good luck,\") which he gave to her', and so came to propose (Leaves from the Journal, 1868); the Queen's private memorandum shows the belief was about already, 'some white heather, which he said was good luck'. She made it fashionable; she didn't invent it. At weddings it followed: 'in some parts of Scotland she would be thought a rash bride who went to church without it' (All the Year Round, as Wallace quotes it), and in 1885 Princess Beatrice's bonnet 'was trimmed with white Scotch heather - which flower in Scotland is considered an omen of good fortune' (The Graphic). 'Lucky white heather' was sold on English streets within living memory, often by Romani women.",
    source: 'Queen Victoria, Leaves from the Journal of Our Life in the Highlands (1868), 29 September 1855 · A. Wallace, The Heather in Lore, Lyric and Lay (1903) · The Graphic (27 July 1885) · NatureScot, "Protected species: plants and fungi" and "Guidance on foraging in Scotland" · National Records of Scotland, "Marriage in Scotland" (RM1)',
    tier: 'record',
    reward: { gold: 60, xp: 40 },
