    resolution: "You don't split a living tree for it. You give her the ash keys to sow by her door instead, since the old belief tied the child's health to the tree's, and a sapling of his own is the gentler half of the charm; and you send her to the surgeon in the town for the rest. The keys come up in spring. So does the boy, mended. Then the practical half. A soft bulge at a baby's belly button is an umbilical hernia: common in babies, it usually goes by itself by the time a child is five. A lump in the groin is an inguinal hernia, and it needs an operation, because a piece of bowel can become trapped; the younger the baby, the greater the risk. Go to A&E straight away, or ring 999, if the lump turns hard, red or tender and can't be pushed back, if he's in sudden severe pain and can't be comforted, if he's being sick, especially green, or if he can't poo or pass wind.",
    lore: "Gilbert White wrote from Selborne in 1776 of 'a row of pollard-ashes, which by the seams and long cicatrices down their sides, manifestly show that, in former times, they have been cleft asunder': 'These trees when young and flexible, were severed and held open by wedges, while ruptured children, stripped naked, were pushed through the apertures'. Then the tree 'was plastered with loam, and carefully swathed up. If the parts coalesced and soldered together, as usually fell out… the party was cured'. 'Several persons now living in the village' had been through it, and White himself, enlarging his garden, 'cut down two or three such trees'. Others held the tree and the child bound for life: at Shirley Heath in Warwickshire a man passed through as an infant 'will not suffer a single branch to be touched, for it is believed the life of the patient depends on the life of the tree' (Gentleman's Magazine, 1804, as Frazer quotes it). In Somerset in 1886 a baby twin 'born with hernia' was passed through 'upon a Sunday morning, before sunrise' (Elworthy).",
