    resolution: "Alder, you tell her: the tree whose cut wood goes from white to orange-red, so that some say it bleeds. Boil the bark and it gives a reddish brown; put iron to it, a little copperas from the druggist, or rusty iron if that's all there is, and the brown goes dark, near black. She dyes her shawl and her mother's in the yard, in an old pan kept for it, and they go to the burying in a dark of their own making. The bowl shows the old man easy, and you tell her so. Then the practical half. If money is short, her mother, as the widow, may get a Funeral Expenses Payment if she's on Universal Credit, Pension Credit, Housing Benefit or income-related ESA: it pays the burial or cremation fees and up to £1,000 more, though not usually the whole cost, and it must be claimed within six months of the funeral; in Scotland it's the Funeral Support Payment instead. If her mother is under State Pension age and her father paid enough National Insurance, there may be Bereavement Support Payment too, best claimed within three months. A funeral can be arranged without a funeral director, and if no one can pay, the council will arrange a simple one. And with the dyeing: keep the pan for dyeing only, and the copperas away from children; it's harmful if swallowed and stings the skin and eyes.",
    lore: "Alder bark was the poor folk's black. Gerard wrote that 'The bark is much used of poor country dyers, for the dying of coarse cloth, caps, hose, and such like into a black colour, whereunto it serveth very well'; Lightfoot, that 'The bark will dye yarn of a fuscous colour', and 'The Highlanders dye their yarn of a black colour, by boiling it with the bark mixed with copperas' (1777); and in 1878 it was said to supply 'the black stripes in homemade tartan'. In Ireland, Joyce wrote, alder gave the 'ground or foundation colour of reddish brown', and the black came from the iron-rich mud of bog pools (1906). Cut alder turns from white to orange-red, and later tellings made it a tree that bled, and an unlucky one. As for mourning, the poor made do with what they had: 'Many dyed and altered garments' (Lou Taylor).",
