    lore: "Crotal, the rock-lichen, was the great brown dye of Highland and Island homespun: in Skye Martin Martin found 'another coarser scurf called crostil. It is of a dark colour' (1703), and in 1927 the Spectator wrote of 'a lichen from the rocks a rich brown, known as crottle'. It needed no mordant; it was scraped off the rocks and boiled with the wool. At sea it was feared. Carmichael's notebook of 1873, as the archive describes it, has sailors who 'never wear cloth made from it as it sinks if they fall out', the 'revenge' of the stones it was taken from; and in 1941 a correspondent told the Scots dictionary of 'a superstition among fishermen on the West Coast that crottal seeks its native rocks', so that they 'were therefore averse to wearing garments dyed with crottal or to taking on board anyone so clad'. Heather gives yellow with alum, and green with iron.",
