    resolution: "The staves are kind. You sit the old man down and between you, with a pot of tea, you put back together what you can of the sea-words — and the ones neither of you can remember, you tell the boy to listen for and never guess at. He'll learn them in the boat, the way they always were learned. Then the practical half, for a boy going to sea, because fishing is still one of the most dangerous trades in the country. Before he sets foot on a working boat he does his Basic Sea Survival course, and first aid, fire-fighting and health and safety within three months of starting. On deck he wears his lifejacket, every time, and the skipper must see that he does. A personal locator beacon on it, registered, so they can find him if he goes over. And if it goes wrong, ring 999 and ask for the Coastguard, or call on Channel 16.",
    lore: "The haaf was the far fishing, for ling and tusk on long lines from six-oared sixareens, thirty or forty miles out. Its men kept a sea language: set words used in place of the ordinary names of \"unlucky\" things, so the church was the bennihoose, the minister the upstander, and the cat a foodin or a krammer. Many come from Norn, \"a Scandinavian language spoken in the isles until the eighteenth century\". Jakob Jakobsen gathered them on his fieldwork of 1893–95, and the first comprehensive glossary was made only in 2023, in Ian Humberstone's Edinburgh thesis. On the night of 20–21 July 1881 a storm caught the haaf boats, and fifty-eight men were drowned, thirty-six of them from Gloup in Yell.",
