    id: 'the_caul',
    caller: 'Mrs Pengelly',
    icon: '🌊',
    title: 'A Caul for Sale',
    plea: "My Davey ships out Monday. There's a woman at the market with a child's caul — born over a baby's face, dried and folded in paper — and she swears no man that carries one ever drowned. Two guineas. That's near everything I have. Should I?",
    steps: [
      { kind: 'scry', label: 'Look in the bowl for the voyage' },
      { kind: 'tarot', label: 'Draw for the Monday sailing' },
    ],
    resolution: "The bowl shows nothing to fear and nothing promised. You tell her the truth as you see it: a caul is a scrap of birth-membrane and a frightened mother's two guineas, and the woman at the market knows it. Spend it on a sound oilskin and a letter for him to carry. She cries a bit, and laughs a bit, and goes to buy the oilskin. Then the practical half, the one that keeps sailors alive. A lifejacket, worn on deck at all times, and looked after: the gas cylinder checked for tightness every month, and the jacket serviced every year. If he goes in, cold water shock comes first, and the seas round our coasts are cold enough for it all year. Float to Live: tilt your head back with your ears under, relax and try to breathe normally, and move your hands and feet to stay afloat; it's fine if your legs sink. Then call, or have someone call, 999 or 112 and ask for the Coastguard. And the Fishermen's Mission keeps a 24-hour emergency and welfare line on 0800 634 1020.",
    lore: "A child born with the caul, a piece of the amniotic membrane, over its face was thought lucky and safe from drowning, and the dried caul was sold to seafarers as a charm against it. The newspapers carried the advertisements for well over a century: in 1779 'To the gentlemen of the Navy, and others going long voyages to sea. To be disposed of, a child's caul'; in 1837 'PRESERVATION at SEA.- To be SOLD a CAUL, on moderate terms'; in 1848 one at six guineas that 'was afloat with its late owner 30 years in all the perils of a seaman's life'. Grose held that the caul told its first owner's health: 'If alive and well, it is firm and crisp; if dead or sick, relaxed and flaccid' (as Thiselton Dyer quotes him, 1881). Dickens opens David Copperfield with the joke: \"I was born with a caul, which was advertised for sale, in the newspapers, at the low price of fifteen guineas.\" The old lady who won it in a raffle 'was never drowned, but died triumphantly in bed, at ninety-two', having never been on the water 'except upon a bridge'.",
