    resolution: "You don't hand anyone to the constable on a sieve's say-so, and you tell her so. Instead you tell her to mention at supper, loudly, that the cunning woman has turned the sieve and knows the name. The spoons are back in the drawer by morning, wrapped in a clean cloth, and nobody says a word about it ever again. Then the practical half. Photograph the silver, a ruler beside each piece, write down its marks and any inscription, and keep any receipts, so you can prove it's yours; register it free on Immobilise, which the police can search when they recover goods. If something is stolen, report it on 101 or online, or ring 999 if it's happening now. And a suspicion isn't evidence: an employer must look into a matter fairly and gather evidence from all sides before acting on it, and Gypsies and Irish Travellers are legally recognised ethnic groups, protected under equality law, so the tinker at the door is no likelier a thief for being one.",
    lore: "The sieve and shears found thieves. 'Stick a pair of Sheers in the rind of a Sieve,' Scot wrote, 'and let two persons set the top of each of their Forefingers upon the upper part of the Sheers, holding it with the Sieve up from the ground steadily, and ask Peter and Paul whether A. B. or C. hath stoln the thing lost, and at the nomination of the guilty person, the Sieve will turn round'; he put the turning down to 'the beating of the pulse' and 'slight of the fingers'. In Ben Jonson's Alchemist (1610) Face throws at Subtle his 'tricks / Of cozening', among them \"searching for things lost, with a sieve and shears\". The sieve crossed to New England: at Andover in 1692 Rebecca Johnson owned to 'the turneing of the sieve', with the words 'By Saint Peter & Saint Paul', and Cotton Mather deplored 'detestable Conjurations, with Sieves, and Keys' (1693, as reprinted). Word that the sieve had been turned could, it was hoped, bring the goods quietly home.",
