    resolution: "You don't take the key's word to the kirk session — men have been hauled before the session for less, and some of them were the ones who turned the key. You tell Rab to say at the market that the Bible's been read over it. The cheeses are back in the byre within the week, a little the worse for the journey. The herrings are not. Then the practical half. A theft goes to the police: ring 101, or 999 if it's happening now or the thief is still about; and if someone knows who did it but won't put their name to it, Crimestoppers takes it on 0800 555 111, and they are never asked who they are. Then make it harder next time: a proper hasp and padlock on the byre door, a light on a timer outside, and photographs and a note of anything worth taking. And never name a man as the thief on the strength of a key or a card: in Scots law, a statement to others that seriously harms someone's good name can be defamation, and a charm is no evidence at all.",
    lore: "The key and Bible found thieves: \"Two neutral persons, sitting opposite each other, held out the forefingers of their right hands\", the Bible hung from them by a key, \"put into the Bible at the fiftieth Psalm\" (Napier, 1879), and at \"When thou sawest a thief, then thou consentedst with him\" (Tylor's wording) it was meant to turn toward the culprit; Brewer gives Ruth i or the 51st Psalm instead. Tylor called it \"still in frequent use\". In 1697 a sack of provisions, cheese and herrings by the museum's account, was taken from a horse's saddle at the tavern at Bankend of Caerlaverock, and John Fergusson of Cummertrees, with \"two holding the bowl of the key upon their fingers\", read the psalm over the names \"till they came to William M'Kinnell\", when \"the key and the Bible turned about and fell on the table\". Fergusson said \"he knew not there was any evil in it\", and the Presbytery of Dumfries appointed him \"to stand on the pillar in the church of Caerlaverock\": it was the charmer the kirk punished, and the record names William M'Kinnell only as the name the key fell at.",
