    resolution: "You knock just after the bells. She has the good glasses out and the black bun cut already. You put the fuel on her fire and the salt on her table, and she gives you Wullie's toast to the New Year, and then she cries a little and then she laughs. The luck, you think, was mostly in someone coming at all. You tell her you will come again next year, and you mean it. Then the practical half, for a first New Year without him. The NHS says plainly that holidays and anniversaries are when grief comes hardest, and that it doesn't come right all at once; grief isn't an illness, but if she's low for more than two weeks together, the GP. Cruse Scotland's bereavement helpline is free on 0808 802 6161, weekdays nine to five and till eight on Monday and Thursday nights. On the nights in between, Samaritans are on 116 123, free, day and night, every day of the year, and Breathing Space on 0800 83 85 87, evenings in the week and all weekend. For the loneliness after, Age Scotland's helpline, 0800 12 44 222 on weekday mornings, can find her a friendship caller, and the Silver Line, 0800 4 70 80 90, is open all hours. And if she ever says she can't go on, ring NHS 24 on 111, or 999.",
